00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.160
So a quick recap on
Kubernetes engine.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.930
Containers address
the problem of how

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.540
to easily and
consistently deploy apps

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.370
in different
environments by allowing

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.550
them to be broken into
smaller, independent pieces.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.060
These can then be deployed
and managed dynamically.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.519
Containerization also
allows the separation

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.130
of apps from infrastructure.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Leveraging
Google's infrastructure,

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.540
Kubernetes engine provides the
perfect managed environment

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.940
for deploying,
managing, and scaling

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.920
your containerized applications.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.600
By simply describing
the compute, memory,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.670
and storage resources your
apps containers require,

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.830
Kubernetes engine automatically
provisions and manages

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.592
the underlying cloud resources.

00:01:27.592 --> 00:01:29.550
ARHAAN SAKSENA: In this
lab, creating a cluster

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.320
on Kubernetes engine will
ensure that it adds a new node

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.770
to your cluster if your pods
don't have enough capacity

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:35.970
to run.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.360
Conversely, if a node in your
cluster is underutilized,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.540
Kubernetes engine
can delete the node.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.450
You'll also use Kubernetes
engine to create a deployment.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.705
[WHOOSH]

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.020
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Bot
